Subject: [PATCH] live disk: the boot volume grows to fill the whole virtual disk

The live image is a 300 MiB BFS partition followed by a 2.8 MiB EFI system
partition. MacRenkuOS boots it from a qcow2 overlay, and /boot stayed
300 MiB with about 10 MiB free whatever size that overlay had -- small
enough that R Chromium's shared memory, which lives in /tmp on the boot
volume, ran out for a single large frame. Shipping a 16 GiB image instead
would put 16 GiB on every download; the overlay's extra space costs nothing
until it is written.

Two halves, because BFS cannot be resized (ResizeVisitor is a stub that
returns B_NOT_SUPPORTED) and a mounted boot volume is no place to start:

- renku_grow_boot, run with --restart by a new renku-grow-boot job in
  data/launch/user's desktop target, recognises the live disk layout (an
  ISO 9660 hybrid whose MBR holds BFS, then the ESP right after it, and
  nothing else), copies the ESP to the end of the disk, verifies the copy,
  rewrites the MBR so the BFS partition reaches up to it, writes
  "grow_to_partition true" to ~/config/settings/kernel/drivers/bfs, says so
  in an alert and restarts. Neither the ESP nor the partition table is in
  use while the system runs. On any other layout, or once the disk is
  grown, it does nothing. (It is called directly rather than through a
  script in /boot/system/boot: files added to "system boot" at the end of
  the minimum image definition do not reach this image -- 0041's
  renku-install-rchromium is missing from it for the same reason.)

- BFS's Volume::Mount(), when that setting is on and the partition is larger
  than the file system, grows the file system before the journal, block
  cache and allocator are set up (_GrowToPartition). The bitmap follows
  block 0 and the log follows the bitmap, and BFS treats everything up to
  the end of the log as reserved -- inode checks and the allocator both
  rely on it -- so the log keeps its end and the larger bitmap takes its
  first blocks: from 300 MiB to 16 GiB with 2 KiB blocks, the log goes from
  2048 to 1546 blocks. Nothing else moves and no block changes hands. This
  needs a clean log; a dirty one, an unexpected layout or a log that would
  drop under 512 blocks leaves the volume as it is. The new bitmap blocks
  are written first (over the clean log only) and the superblock last, so
  an interruption leaves the old volume intact.

The boot loader hands ~/config/settings/kernel/drivers/* to the kernel, so
the setting reaches BFS before the boot volume is mounted.

